**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the complete polymorphism and relevant mineral descriptions; [Interpret vapor pressure, phase change, and a phase diagram](../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Phase-changes-and-equilibrium-maps.lingua#r_7XXDG9YQTWEGSDFX1HXPBTGY8S), the full shared phase-change explanation; [Petrology — Dexter Perkins, open textbook](../sources/Petrology-Perkins.lingua#r_6P7JBVJJ3P1BT6QKDGC7BN6VWV), the complete relevant phase/indicator-mineral treatment.

Polymorphs share a chemical composition while differing in structure. Their stability depends on conditions and the adopted thermodynamic description. A structural transformation can change density, optical response, mechanical behavior, and reaction pathways without changing the ideal bulk elemental formula.

Equilibrium phase boundaries compare Gibbs energies under defined conditions. Observing a phase outside a simple stability field can reflect kinetic persistence, compositional effects, uncertainty, a mistaken identification, or a model limitation. The diagram alone does not determine which explanation applies.

Transitions can involve distinct mechanisms and barriers. Nucleation, diffusion, defects, strain, grain size, fluids, and time influence the preserved result. A mineral interpreted as a pressure/temperature indicator needs evidence that the relevant structure formed or equilibrated during the event being inferred, rather than being inherited or retrogressed.

**Work:** compare a familiar polymorph pair and the Al₂SiO₅ system using the full mineral/phase treatments. Distinguish composition, structure, stability field, experimental calibration, and natural texture. Propose an alternative history for a preserved phase association and identify which observation could distinguish it.

Read the full polymorphism and phase-change discussion. The later metamorphic and experimental studies deepen phase relations and kinetics. This step establishes why chemical analysis alone cannot distinguish every mineral or justify a unique pressure-temperature interpretation.

